#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096445 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d5c96d-ca13-5c1e-83d3-0dc9f6da0f83" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"}] :question/id #uuid "069b2882-f4a9-589f-a903-4e89d077cd85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4279" :question/problem "Suppose, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Given that $7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda \\left(3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b} \\right)$ are equal vectors, find the scalar factor $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}= \\dfrac{7}{3} (3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc49f831-8e1d-5fb4-8952-1dd4ee3bfa02" :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "d1329498-4413-5d87-a814-be0d57a3afd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4280" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "11c09a26-0e83-5a77-b777-aea8a3594960" :question/math-academy-id "e-5183" :question/problem "Consider the diagram that is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{c}$\n2. $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$\n3. $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{e}$\n\n![](images/95/95ae711c1ccbf7543e6d269aa8d817a66e2ac348113ea71f3a1babcbb23d1ca3.png)" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ have different magnitudes. So, $\\mathbf{b} \\neq -\\mathbf{c}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Statement III is false. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is greater than the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq 2\\mathbf{e}$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}]}
